Do not take a run of slashes for a UNC prefix in dirname().
Any path starting with two separators was parsed as a UNC path, even
when no host name followed. The whole of "//" or "///usr" became the
UNC prefix, and dirname() appended a separator to it, so "dirname //"
printed "///" and "dirname ///usr" printed "///usr/". Treat the start
as UNC only when the third character is neither a separator nor the
end, so that such paths have the root as their directory.

---
 win32/dirname.c |    6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/win32/dirname.c
+++ b/win32/dirname.c
@@ -101,8 +101,10 @@
     info->base_sep_end = NULL;
     info->term_sep_begin = NULL;
 
-    if(IS_DIR_SEP(pos[0]) && IS_DIR_SEP(pos[1])) {
-      /* The path is UNC.  */
+    if(IS_DIR_SEP(pos[0]) && IS_DIR_SEP(pos[1])
+       && pos[2] != 0 && !IS_DIR_SEP(pos[2])) {
+      /* The path is UNC. Two separators alone, or three or more, are
+       * just a root.  */
       pos += 2;
 
       /* Seek to the end of the share/device name.  */
